<!-- Section 5: Interview Defense Q&A (Technical Grilling Simulator) -->
<div class="flex flex-col gap-space-md">
<div class="flex items-end justify-between">
<div>
<span class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-secondary tracking-widest flex items-center gap-1">
<span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-secondary"></span> The Grilling Room
          </span>
<h2 class="font-headline-lg text-headline-lg text-on-surface mt-0.5">Interview Defense Q&amp;A Blueprint</h2>
</div>
<span class="font-code-sm text-code-sm text-outline hidden md:block">Expected in Google L4 / Meta E4 Systems Loops</span>
</div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-space-md">
<!-- Q&A Card 1 -->
<div class="bg-surface-container rounded-xl p-space-lg flex flex-col justify-between shadow-lg">
<div>
<div class="flex items-center gap-space-xs text-error font-code-sm text-code-sm font-semibold mb-space-sm">
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-base">warning</span>
<span>Question 01 • Safety Invariant</span>
</div>
<h3 class="font-headline-sm text-headline-sm text-on-surface">
              "How do you handle Split-Brain when a partitioned former leader keeps taking writes?"
            </h3>
<div class="mt-space-md space-y-space-sm text-on-surface-variant font-body-md text-body-md">
<p>
<strong class="text-on-surface">The Defense:</strong> Raft completely eliminates split-brain by requiring a strict majority quorum (<code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">N/2 + 1</code>) before committing any entry to the log.
              </p>
<p>
                If an old leader is isolated in a minority partition (e.g., 2 nodes out of 5), it will accept client writes conditionally, but its <code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">AppendEntries</code> RPCs will never gain quorum approval. It cannot advance its <code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">commitIndex</code>.
              </p>
<div class="p-space-sm bg-surface-container-lowest rounded-lg font-code-sm text-code-sm text-tertiary">
                &gt; Follow-up nuance: Implement Leader Leases or ReadIndex verification to ensure linearizable stale-read prevention during transient partitions.
              </div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mt-space-md pt-space-sm flex items-center justify-between text-code-sm font-code-sm text-outline">
<span>Interview Frequency: Very High</span>
<span class="text-primary font-bold">100% Core</span>
</div>
</div>
<!-- Q&A Card 2 -->
<div class="bg-surface-container rounded-xl p-space-lg flex flex-col justify-between shadow-lg">
<div>
<div class="flex items-center gap-space-xs text-secondary font-code-sm text-code-sm font-semibold mb-space-sm">
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-base">psychology</span>
<span>Question 02 • Architectural Tradeoffs</span>
</div>
<h3 class="font-headline-sm text-headline-sm text-on-surface">
              "Why did you choose Go over C++ or Rust for this consensus implementation?"
            </h3>
<div class="mt-space-md space-y-space-sm text-on-surface-variant font-body-md text-body-md">
<p>
<strong class="text-on-surface">The Defense:</strong> Go provides first-class primitives for CSP concurrency (<code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">goroutines</code>, <code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">channels</code>, and <code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">select</code>), which directly maps to Raft's asynchronous state machine timers and RPC heartbeats.
              </p>
<p>
                While Rust provides zero-cost abstractions without GC pauses, Go allowed us to hit 4.8ms p99 write latency by pre-allocating byte slice memory pools (<code class="font-code-sm text-secondary">sync.Pool</code>) to avoid GC thrashing while drastically accelerating development velocity.
              </p>
<div class="p-space-sm bg-surface-container-lowest rounded-lg font-code-sm text-code-sm text-secondary">
                &gt; Demonstrates pragmatic engineering judgment rather than dogmatic language partisanship.
              </div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mt-space-md pt-space-sm flex items-center justify-between text-code-sm font-code-sm text-outline">
<span>Interview Frequency: High</span>
<span class="text-primary font-bold">Design Tradeoff</span>
</div>
</div>
<!-- Q&A Card 3 -->
<div class="bg-surface-container rounded-xl p-space-lg flex flex-col justify-between shadow-lg">
<div>
<div class="flex items-center gap-space-xs text-tertiary font-code-sm text-code-sm font-semibold mb-space-sm">
<span class="material-symbols-outlined text-base">memory</span>
<span>Question 03 • Production Longevity</span>
</div>
<h3 class="font-headline-sm text-headline-sm text-on-surface">
              "How does your log compaction avoid memory exhaustion without blocking incoming writes?"
            </h3>
<div class="mt-space-md space-y-space-sm text-on-surface-variant font-body-md text-body-md">
<p>
<strong class="text-on-surface">The Defense:</strong> We decoupled snapshot generation using Copy-on-Write (CoW) semantics on the underlying in-memory Skip-List. 
              </p>
<p>
                When a snapshot threshold is hit (e.g. 50,000 entries), a background worker acquires a brief read-lock to clone the root pointer, then asynchronously serializes the snapshot to an mmap disk buffer without stalling the active leader's gRPC ingest path.
              </p>
<div class="p-space-sm bg-surface-container-lowest rounded-lg font-code-sm text-code-sm text-tertiary">
                &gt; Mention InstallSnapshot streaming via chunked gRPC streams for nodes that rejoin after extended outages.
              </div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mt-space-md pt-space-sm flex items-center justify-between text-code-sm font-code-sm text-outline">
<span>Interview Frequency: High</span>
<span class="text-primary font-bold">Storage Internals</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
